{"id": "43212ba2-d9cd-44f9-a5f9-9810b4fc9001", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:13:34.985515", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "efd619b7-0530-4ca3-9267-2e1264c84d79", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Environment fully characterized:\n- /workspace contains only task.json (579 bytes). No QuaRot code, no quantization tooling, no model definitions, no calibration data, no eval harness.\n- No track.md file on disk; only a \"documents\" entry in task.json with the note \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"\n- Python 3.14.4, no torch/transformers/numpy/scipy/datasets/tqdm. No network. No GPU. 224 CPUs, ~2TB RAM, but /workspace only has ~12G free (99% used); /tmp is 1TB tmpfs.\n\nNow, the researcher asks for source-supported setup facts about QuaRot (researcher_topics refer to NeurIPS 2024 paper 94328 = QuaRot: \"QuaRot: Quality Quadruple... Actually QuaRot: \"QuaRot: 2D Qu... \"QuaRot: Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs\" (NeurIPS 2024). Topics: setup_5, setup_7, setup_11, setup_16, setup_17 — I don't have access to those documents. I should state facts that are supported by the public QuaRot paper as knowledge, and clearly label them as paper-level facts vs. my proposed choices. But careful: the researcher asked for \"QuaRot ke source-supported basic setup facts ka summary: weight-only quantization, W bits ∈ {4,3,2}, aur comparison baselines RTN (bina calibration) tatha GPTQ (Hessian-approximation wala error-minimizing method)\".\n\nI should be careful about what the paper actually says vs. what the task premise says. The QuaRot paper's headline is 4-bit weight + 4-bit activation + 4-bit KV cache (W4A4KV4), and it explicitly discusses weight-only quantization (W4, W3, W2) as an important setting because of memory-bandwidth-limited inference. The paper's baselines for weight-only quantization include RTN and GPTQ (as in OmniQuant/AWQ comparisons). The paper states that at 2-bit, GPTQ degrades severely, while QuaRot (Hadamard rotation) retains quality. Also, paper reports perplexity on WikiText-2 and C4 with sequence length 2048 (sliding-window?), and zero-shot tasks: PIQA, ARC-easy/ARC-challenge, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, plus LAMBADA and MMLU for larger models. Models: LLaMA-2 and LLaMA-3 families, sizes 7B/13B/70B and LLaMA-3 8B/70B, plus Falcon and Mistral possibly. Group quantization with group size 128 for weight-only quantization; per-group scale/zero-point (asymmetric). Hadamard: randomized Hadamard transform applied offline to weights (equivalence transformation H H^T = I), and online Hadamard for activations (had_all/X) — the \"rotation\" pushes outliers to be Gaussian-like so quantization error drops. Key theory claim: quantization error bound is related to the coherence / max-magnitude, and random rotation minimizes the coherence and makes weights/activations approximately Gaussian, achieving rate-distortion-optimal quantization (per paper's theorem/analysis in Section on quantization noise).\n\nImportant detail regarding weight-only: QuaRot uses offline rotation only for the query/key projection inputs? Actually the paper's design: for weight-only quantization, all rotations are folded into the weights offline (X H (H^T W)... ). They note that with weight-only quantization, no online compute is needed at all. Also, they mention for W-only, they use \"randomized Hadamard\" vs \"Hadamard\"? I believe the paper uses Hadamard matrices (fast Walsh-Hadamard), and ablates the random sign flip (\"randomized Hadamard\"). Hmm, in QuaRot they mention \"we use a random Hadamard matrix H_R = H ⊙ signs\"? Let me be careful and mark these as \"to verify against source\".\n\nSince the topic IDs are opaque to me and I cannot open the paper, I should state facts at a confidence level and flag which need researcher verification. The researcher explicitly said don't call choices \"facts\". Good.\n\nI should also mention: QuaRot paper reports that for weight-only quantization at W4/W3/W2 with group size 128 on LLaMA-2 70B etc., QuaRot substantially outperforms RTN/GPTQ, and at 2-bit GPTQ often fails (perplexity blow-up) while QuaRot remains near FP16. Also QuaRot 2-bit weight-only on LLaMA-2 7B... I'll present these as \"paper-level claims to be verified\", not exact numbers (I shouldn't invent numbers).\n\nAlso I can run a tiny numerical sanity check that demonstrates feasibility of a CPU-only, numpy-free... hmm, numpy isn't installed. So no numerical checks possible. I could do pure-Python simulation of Hadamard rotation + quantization error on synthetic outlier-heavy matrices using only stdlib (math, random, cmath). That's feasible and could serve as a \"feasible check\" in the design: a stdlib-only toy simulation demonstrating that random Hadamard rotation reduces max-magnitude/coherence and hence reduces MSE of uniform quantization. That's a nice, honest, runnable-in-this-environment artifact for a design-only task. But the researcher only wants the plan now (3 items). I'll mention it as an optional feasible check within the approach, not execute now... Actually, I could execute a quick stdlib check now to add evidence that the design's toy-simulation leg is feasible in this environment. That strengthens evidence without claiming an experiment. Let me do a small one — cheap and quick (pure Python, ~small matrices). That's within budget and honest: \"feasibility probe of a toy simulation, not an experiment result\".\n\nLet me write a small script: generate synthetic weight matrices with outliers, apply Hadamard (Sylvester construction for power-of-2 dims, with random sign flips), quantize with symmetric per-group uniform quantization at 4/3/2 bits, compute MSE and (optionally) relative error. Compare no-rotation vs rotation. Also simulate GPTQ-ish? No — GPTQ requires Hessian; skip. Just show that rotation reduces quantization MSE, which supports the mechanism leg of the design.\n\nI'll do it quickly with pure stdlib. Sizes: 512x512 maybe heavy in pure Python (262k elements, times log n ops). Fine, do 256x256 with a few groups. Use fast Walsh–Hadamard transform in pure Python: 256*8 = 2048 ops per row, 256 rows → trivial.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nNote: pure python random with seed. Compute per-group MSE where groups are contiguous chunks along input dimension (like group quantization along in-features). Quantization: symmetric, scale = max|w_group| / (2^(b-1)-1).\n\nMetric: total MSE, and also \"coherence\"/max-magnitude ratio.\n\nLet's write /tmp/hadamard_probe.py and run it.\n\nThen produce the plan via end_researcher_turn stage=plan with the three requested sections. Language: researcher wrote in Hinglish. I'll respond in Hinglish/English mix (Hinglish with technical English terms) — appropriate. Keep structured.\n\nLet me do the probe now.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "mkdir -p /tmp/probe && cat > /tmp/probe/had.py <<'EOF'\n# Pure-stdlib feasibility probe (NO numpy/torch needed): does a Hadamard rotation\n# reduce uniform-quantization MSE for outlier-heavy matrices at 4/3/2 bits?\nimport random, math\n\ndef fwhm(v):           # fast Walsh-Hadamard transform, in place, normalized\n    n=len(v); h=1\n    while h<n:\n        for i in range(0,n,h*2):\n            for j in range(i,i+h):\n                a,b=v[j],v[j+h]; v[j]=a+b; v[j+h]=a-b\n        h*=2\n    s=1.0/math.sqrt(n)\n    for i in range(n): v[i]*=s\n\ndef quant_mse(row, bits, g):\n    tot=0.0\n    for s in range(0,len(row),g):\n        blk=row[s:s+g]\n        m=max(max(blk),-min(blk))\n        q=max(m,1e-8)/(2**(bits-1)-1)\n        for x in blk:\n            r=round(x/q)*q\n            tot+=(x-r)**2\n    return tot/len(row)\n\ndef make(n, out_frac=0.01, out_scale=30.0, seed=0):\n    rnd=random.Random(seed)\n    return [[rnd.gauss(0,1)+(out_scale*rnd.choice([1,-1]) if rnd.random()<out_frac else 0)\n             for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\n\nn,g=256,128\nfor bits in (4,3,2):\n    a=b=c=0.0; mx_a=mx_b=0.0\n    for seed in range(5):\n        W=make(n,seed=seed)\n        for r in range(n):\n            row=W[r]\n            a+=quant_mse(row,bits,g)\n            mx_a=max(mx_a,max(map(abs,row)))\n            yr=[-x if (seed*7+r)%3==0 else x for x in row]          # random sign flip\n            fwhm(yr)                                                # Hadamard rotation\n            b+=quant_mse(yr,bits,g)\n            mx_b=max(mx_b,max(map(abs,yr)))\n    print(f\"bits={bits}  MSE(no-rot)={a/(5*n):.5f}  MSE(Had)={b/(5*n):.5f}  ratio={b/a:.3f}  \"\n          f\"max|w| no-rot={mx_a:.1f} Had={mx_b:.1f}\")\nEOF\ntime python3 /tmp/probe/had.py", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b0c8fc94fc615026", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-b0c8fc94fc615026", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"mkdir -p /tmp/probe && cat > /tmp/probe/had.py <<'EOF'\\n# Pure-stdlib feasibility probe (NO numpy/torch needed): does a Hadamard rotation\\n# reduce uniform-quantization MSE for outlier-heavy matrices at 4/3/2 bits?\\nimport random, math\\n\\ndef fwhm(v):           # fast Walsh-Hadamard transform, in place, normalized\\n    n=len(v); h=1\\n    while h<n:\\n        for i in range(0,n,h*2):\\n            for j in range(i,i+h):\\n                a,b=v[j],v[j+h]; v[j]=a+b; v[j+h]=a-b\\n        h*=2\\n    s=1.0/math.sqrt(n)\\n    for i in range(n): v[i]*=s\\n\\ndef quant_mse(row, bits, g):\\n    tot=0.0\\n    for s in range(0,len(row),g):\\n        blk=row[s:s+g]\\n        m=max(max(blk),-min(blk))\\n        q=max(m,1e-8)/(2**(bits-1)-1)\\n        for x in blk:\\n            r=round(x/q)*q\\n            tot+=(x-r)**2\\n    return tot/len(row)\\n\\ndef make(n, out_frac=0.01, out_scale=30.0, seed=0):\\n    rnd=random.Random(seed)\\n    return [[rnd.gauss(0,1)+(out_scale*rnd.choice([1,-1]) if rnd.random()<out_frac else 0)\\n             for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\\n\\nn,