{"id": "991554d2-b637-43ea-81b5-19826754ffc7", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:14:26.580484", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "cdd66adb-c4d7-4323-9330-957a567d24f2", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Interesting — the toy probe shows ratio 0.143 at 4 bits, 0.692 at 3 bits, but 5.2 at 2 bits (worse). Why worse at 2 bits? Because at 2 bits symmetric with only 3 levels (±1, 0... actually 2**(2-1)-1 = 1, so levels {-1,0,1} × q) — the rotation spreads outliers into all coordinates making them all non-zero, and with 1 mantissa level, rounding to ±1 q with q = max/1, most values round to 0 → catastrophic. This is a known toy-model artifact: symmetric vs asymmetric quantization, and 2-bit typically uses asymmetric (min/max) quantization with 4 levels and per-group scales, which matters a lot. Let me refine: use asymmetric min/max quantization (as in QuaRot weight-only: group-wise asymmetric quantization à la GPTQ with zero-points) and re-run. Also the rotation applied per-row (independently per output channel) is not exactly QuaRot (which applies a single shared rotation in the input dimension). But for MSE-per-weight purposes the effect of outlier smoothing is captured. The important honest takeaway for design: at 2-bit, the *quantizer grid/level count and asymmetric zero-point* choice interacts strongly with rotation, so the design must pin down group size and symmetric-vs-asymmetric, and the toy probe should mirror the real quantizer (asymmetric, per-group of 128).\n\nLet me re-run with asymmetric quantization to check whether the 2-bit inversion disappears. This is a good, honest feasibility check that yields design constraints.\n\nNote about the probe's per-row independent random sign flips — fine.\n\nLet me test asymmetric.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat > /tmp/probe/had2.py <<'EOF'\n# Same probe but with ASYMMETRIC per-group quantization (min/max + zero-point, as used by\n# GPTQ/QuaRot weight-only), and also com