{"id": "c641dd35-0cd7-4020-80a3-b1cf7dbb339c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:16:17.777936", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "5bf5a3c7-fb2a-45ce-b68f-43ae033dee05", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Network blocked; pip install fails. Confirmed.\n\nCheck persistence of /workspace across calls in the next call.\n\nNow let me also consider: /workspace available space is only 12G — a real run of LLaMA-2/3 7B fp16 (13-15GB) would not fit in /workspace, but /tmp has ~1TB tmpfs. That's a useful feasibility observation, but /tmp is wiped between calls (observed). I'll note both.\n\nNow compose the plan response in Hinglish/English mix as requested, with three sections:\n\n1) Inspection findings + QuaRot source-supported setup facts.\n2) Proposed approach outline (high-level only).\n3) Assumptions/uncertainties, no \"we ran it\" claims.\n\nOn QuaRot facts — I must be careful to label what's source-supported from the paper (QuaRot: Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs, Saleh Ashkboos et al., NeurIPS 2024) at the level of general knowledge, and mark anything I can't verify. The researcher gave topics IDs (setup_5/7/11/16/17) but no documents were actually provided in the workspace — only track.md's stub string in task.json. So I must state that I have no access to the paper text here; facts I state are from my prior knowledge of the paper and should be verified by researcher / against the paper. That's an honest and important distinction.\n\nFacts (prior knowledge, to be verified):\n- QuaRot applies an exact (equivalence-preserving) rotation of the LLM using Hadamard transformations: X W = (X H)(H^T W), with H orthogonal; rotations are folded into weights offline for weight-only quantization (no extra online cost); for activations some Hadamard transforms must be applied online (e.g., input activations to down_proj / residual-to-attention rotation) — in the paper's W4A4 they keep online Hadamard at two spots.\n- Weight-only setting: since only weights are quantized, all rotations can be absorbed offline; the online network is unchanged, so latency overhead is zero (only accuracy effect).\n- Quantization grid: group-wise (group size 128) quantization with scale/zero-point, per output channel; W bits 4, 3, 2 evaluated in weight-only experiments; models: LLaMA-2 7B/13B/70B, LLaMA-3 8B/70B.\n- Baselines in the weight-only tables: RTN (round-to-nearest, no calibration) and GPTQ (layer-wise second-order/Hessian-based error compensation using calibration data); also OmniQuant/AWQ appear in some tables of related work.\n- Evaluation: WikiText-2 perplexity and C4 perplexity with sequence length 2048; zero-shot tasks (PIQA, ARC-e, ARC-c, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, LAMBADA, sometimes MMLU); accuracy reported as average.\n- Paper's reported finding (as prior knowledge): QuaRot keeps perplexity close to FP16 at W4 and degrades gracefully at W3/W2, whereas GPTQ/RTN degrade sharply, most severely at W2.\n- Theory section: quantization error is bounded by kurtosis / max-magnitude; Hadamard rotation minimizes max-magnitude outliers and pushes weights/activations toward Gaussian (high-dim central limit), lowering noise; also rotation preserves inner products (orthogonal) → equivalence.\n\nI'll flag each of these as \"prior-knowledge, needs verification against the paper text\" since the doc isn't available locally.\n\nAlso note: the current environment has NO access to the paper, so the design must specify that numbers come from the paper only via researcher-provided text; otherwise they are assumptions.\n\nNow for the design outline:\n\nModels: LLaMA-3.1-8B + LLaMA-2-7B/13B/70B (paper-comparable); smaller proxy for feasibility: LLaMA-3.2-1B/3B or TinyLlama for cheap sanity arms; also Qwen2.5-7B for family generalization (but flag as outside paper scope).\n\nMethod arms (2×2 + baselines):\n- A. FP16/BF16 reference (no quant) — anchor.\n- B. RTN (no calibration), group-wise, g=128.\n- C. GPTQ (calibration, Hessian-based), g=128.\n- D. RTN + Hadamard rotation (offline folded).\n- E. GPTQ + Hadamard rotation.\nOptional: OmniQuant/AWQ as extra reference; SmoothQuant-style scaling as an alternative \"non-Hadamard\" outlier mitigation → a mechanism-isolating control.\n\nPrecision points: W4/W3/W2, group sizes {128 (paper default), 64, 32} + one per-channel (g = full row) to test the interaction between rotation benefit and group granularity. Symmetric vs asymmetric as a design detail (flagged: must match paper).\n\nRotation variants/ablation: Hadamard (deterministic) vs random-Hadamard (random signs before H); which rotation matrices are applied (offline-only for weight-only: W ← W H for all linear layers, X-rotation folded into embeddings/LN; and the \"online\" ones set to identity in weight-only). Plus rotation scale γ (learnable/online diagonal scaling, e.g., per-channel scaling before rotation) → ablate γ=1 vs tuned. Also a control \"wrong rotation\" (random orthogonal non-Hadamard, same orthogonality but no structured fast transform) to test whether the effect is Hadamard-specific or generic to orthogonal rotations. Important mechanism control: permutation-only or scaling-only baselines.\n\nMetrics (quality of weight-only quantized model):\n- Primary: WikiText-2 perplexity (seq len 2048), C4 perplexity.\n- Secondary: zero-shot accuracy: PIQA, ARC-e/c, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, LAMBADA (avg); optionally MMLU for 7B+.\n- Mechanism/process metrics: per-layer weight quantization MSE (relative), max|w|/RMS ratio (outlier metric), kurtosis of rotated weights, layer-wise output MSE vs FP16 (per-layer proxy), activation outlier stats before/after rotation.\n- Degradation metric: ΔPPL relative to FP16 and % of FP16 accuracy retained; \"survival threshold\" — lowest bits at which PPL stays < 2× FP16 or accuracy ≥ X.\n- Optional runtime/memory: model size in GB, weight-only rotation is offline, so online latency should equal FP16 modulo dequant kernel — measure to confirm zero online overhead.\n\nControls/ablations:\n1. Hadamard applied to weights only vs weights+input-activation rotation folding (weight-only: all folding offline; but ablate \"partial folding\" — e.g., rotating only MLP vs all layers) → tests where the benefit comes from.\n   Note KV/activation quantization is out of scope for weight-only, but include one arm W-only + A4/KV4 as \"context\" only if researcher wants to connect to W4A4 headline; otherwise explicitly out of scope.\n2. Group size interaction (128 vs 32 vs per-channel) — my toy probe shows the Hadamard MSE gain shrinks as group shrinks; must be a first-class ablation, and this is a genuine design prediction.\n3. Calibration set/size for GPTQ: C4 vs WikiText (never eval on calibration!), nsamples 128 vs 512, seq 2048; GPTQ act-order and dampening (percdamp 0.01/0.1/0.25) — Hessian damping matters at 2 bits.\n4. Seed/variance: random-Hadamard sign seeds (≥3 seeds) for both quant arms; report mean±std and paired tests; note that FP16 PPL is deterministic (seeds only affect rotation + GPTQ tie-breaking).\n5. Symmetric vs asymmetric quant, round-to-nearest with clipping search (MSE-optimal clipping) — disentangles \"rotation benefit\" from \"clipping benefit\".\n6. Non-Hadamard orthogonal rotation (random orthogonal) control.\n7. No-outlier synthetic control (already demonstrated in my probe) for mechanism sanity.\n8. Sanity check on equivalence: quantize with 16-bit after rotation → PPL must equal FP16 (verifies the rotation is exact/equivalence-preserving and the harness is correct). This is a critical correctness gate.\n\nStatistics: paired comparisons on the same eval sequences; report ΔPPL with bootstrap CI over document windows; multiple seeds; effect size. Pre-register success criterion for \"QuaRot stabilizes 2-bit\": e.g., at W2 g=128, GPTQ PPL ≥ 10× FP16 vs QuaRot-GPTQ ≤ 2× FP16, and zero-shot avg within ~5pt of FP16.\n\nFeasibility in this env: cannot run; but a stdlib-only synthetic simulation IS runnable here (demonstrated). Design should include it as \"Tier-0 mechanism simulation\" deliverable: synthetic outlier-heavy weights + RTN vs GPTQ-analog (implement simple Hessian-free greedy / least-squares layer-wise quantization in pure Python) + Hadamard variants, measuring MSE and (on a tiny trained MLP toy model) task loss degradation. That gives real evidence within the environment constraints. Note pure-Python GPTQ at scale is too slow; keep toy dims.\n\nCompute budget estimate for the real experiment (if GPU were available): arms × models × bits — roughly 5 arms × 3 model sizes × 3 bits = 45 quant runs + 3 FP16 refs; GPTQ on 7B ≈ tens of minutes to a few hours on one A100; PPL eval cheap; zero-shot 6 tasks moderate. Note 70B needs 140GB+ for FP16 → 8×80GB or offload; propose dropping 70B to a smaller matrix or run 70B only at W4/W2 for top-line.\n\nNow let me verify persistence check, then hand off plan. Keep the answer reasonably concise but complete — the researcher asked for outline only, so don't over-detail. I'll include the key numbers from probes.\n\nLet me do the persistence check call now (single call).\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "cat /workspace/.persist_probe 2>&1; ls -la /workspace; ls /tmp/p 2>&1 | head -2", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8dc538710827ac57", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-8dc538710827ac57", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"cat /workspace/.persist_probe 2>&1; ls -la /workspace; ls /tmp/p 2>&1 | head -2\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-d10b556d-db16-9f54-b616-5767ac8c8ff6", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "execute_check: {\"command\": \"cat /workspace/.persist_probe 2>&1; ls -la /workspace; ls /tmp/p 2>&1 | head -2\"}", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
