more meaningful: compute the end-to-end weight reconstruction error after rotating, quantizing, and unrotating — that's the real weight-only pipeline, and it shows the rotation reduces effective weight reconstruction error in the original basis. That's a good toy to add: it shows benefit measured in the ORIGINAL basis (which is what matters for weight-only, since output error = X(W - Ŵ)).\n\nThat's an important refinement: the correct metric for weight-only quantization with rotation is not MSE in the rotated basis but ||W - dequant(Q(WH))Hᵀ||_F, and even better, the Hessian-weighted norm ||X(W - Ŵ)||_F which connects to GPTQ's objective. I can implement a Hessian-weighted error metric in the toy probe: E = tr((W-Ŵ) H (W-Ŵ)ᵀ) with H = XᵗX. And show a crude GPTQ-like (error-compensating) scheme vs RTN within the toy, to illustrate that GPTQ helps at 4-bit and collapses at 2-bit while rotation helps more at 2-bit. That would strengthen the design's mechanism narrative but risks being too much like \"running an experiment\". It's a synthetic illustration, clearly labeled. I have step budget (60 steps, used ~10). Let me do 2-3 probes:\n\nProbe A: rotation-then-quantize-then-unrotate in original basis, MSE at 4/3/2 bits, group 128 asymmetric — this is the correct weight-only metric.\nProbe B: dose-response with outlier fraction/scale sweep → negative control + monotonicity check.\nProbe C: crude GPTQ-ish error compensation vs RTN under rotation and non-rotation, with Hessian-weighted error, to pre-register the trend and show where naive 2-bit fails. Keep simple: implement greedy \"OBQ\"-style: quantize weights sequentially along input dim, propagate residual error to remaining columns (error compensation using H inverse diagonal). That's a reasonable GPTQ-lite. Might be slow in pure python for 256x256 with 5 seeds; 256*256*256 = 16.7M ops per matrix — maybe a few seconds each; reduce n to 128 and 2 seeds. Let's try.\n\nAlso, importantly, note the group dimension issue: in the real model, groups are along input dimension, and Hadamard rotation is along the input dimension too — so quantization groups after rotation span mixed original features. My toy does this per-row, which matches per-output-channel rows and group along input dim. Good.\n\nAlso compute-arm count estimate table for feasibility section.\n\nLet me write probe A+B first.\n\nOne more thing: I should double-check whether the Hadamard rotation in the real model is applied per-head for some matrices (V/O and K/Q rotations). QuaRot: rotations are applied for all weight matrices, with online Hadamard for down_proj (in the original paper, since MLP down projection's input can't be rotated offline because of non-linearity). For weight-only, no online needed at all. Also QuaRot uses per-head Hadamard for query/key rotations for KV cache (in W4KV4). Also uses random Hadamard matrices (H_r). These are assumptions to confirm — fine, mark them as requirement-to-confirm.\n\nLet me now run the probes.\n\nAlso careful: /tmp isn't persistent; each execute_check needs self-contained script. Fine.\n\nLet me write probe A+B in one call, and probe C (GPTQ-lite) in another. They're independent — could do in parallel in one message. Yes, two independent calls in same block.\n\nProbe A: correct weight-only metric in the original basis + negative control dose response.\n\nLet me code carefully.\n\ndef quant_asym(vec, bits, g) -> returns dequantized list.\nFor rotation: row of W (length n, along input dim) rotated: w H ⊗ I... Actually for a linear layer W ∈ R^{out×in}, the rotation is W' = W H where H ∈ R^{in×in}. Row-wise: each row of W multiplied by H from right → row_h = H^T applied to row? For symmetric Hadamard (H = H^T), just FWHM on the row. Then quantize W' in groups along the in-dim. Then unrotate: Ŵ = Ŵ' H^T. Error in original basis: ||Ŵ - W||².\n\nImportant subtlety: the unrotation is exact (FP), so the effective error is (Q(WH) - WH)H^T, and since H orthogonal, Frobenius norm is preserved: ||Ŵ-W||_F = ||Ŵ' - W'||_F. So MSE in original basis = MSE in rotated basis for Frobenius. Right — orthogonal invariance. So the Frobenius metric doesn't change; but the Hessian-weighted metric ||X(Ŵ-W)||² DOES matter and depends on rotation of X too. Since X is also rotated (X̂ = XH), the equivalent objective is with rotated activations: error = (X̂)(Ŵ'-W') = X(Ŵ-W). So it's the same objective in rotated coordinates with Hessian H' = HᵀXH... i.e., H' = Hᵀ XᵗX H. Under the standard \"isotropic-ish\" assumption after rotation, Hessian-weighted error ≈ scalar × Frobenius, which is precisely QuaRot's theoretical argument: GPTQ's Hessian-weighted objective becomes ≈ plain MSE after rotation, so RTN becomes near-optimal and GPTQ's advantage disappears. That's a great design-level insight to include: one predicted side effect is that in the rotated space, RTN ≈ GPTQ (gap closes) because the Hessian becomes near-diagonal/isotropic. And I can demonstrate this in the toy probe: compute the \"anisotropy\" of the Hessian (e.g., ratio of trace² / (n·||H||_F²) or condition number) before and after rotation. That's a very concrete mechanism metric: Hessian eigenvalue spread shrinks after rotation → error-minimizing methods have less to gain.\n\nSo probe C: \n- Construct X with heavy outlier channels (like real LLM activations), H = XᵗX, and weights with their own outliers.\n- Metric: Hessian-weighted output error ||X(W-Ŵ)||² for RTN and GPTQ-lite, with and without rotation (rotating both X and W consistently).\n- Also report Hessian anisotropy ratio before/after rotation.\n\nThis will be a nice illustrative table for the design doc's mechanism section, clearly labeled synthetic.\n\nCareful with cost: pure Python with n=96 or 128 dims, 2 seeds. X: 512 samples × 128 dims; W: 128×128 (out=128). Computing X(W-Ŵ) is 512*128*128 = 8.4M mults per arm per bit → in Python maybe 3-8 s. Times arms (RTN, GPTQ-lite) × bits (3) × rot (2) × seeds (2) = 36 → too slow. Reduce: samples=128, out=64, in=128: 128*64*128=1M per eval, 36 evals = 36M ops ≈ 30-60s. GPTQ-lite inner loop is more expensive: per output row, per input column (128), error propagation over remaining columns (128) → 128*128*128 = 2M per row × 64 rows = 132M per matrix per bit... too slow. Simplify: apply GPTQ-lite on a single layer with out=32, in=128: 32*128*128=0.5M per matrix-bit combo; ok.\n\nActually maybe just do the Hessian anisotropy metric (cheap) + output-error metric with RTN only + a cheap \"error-feedback along rows\" GPTQ-lite on one moderate matrix. Let's try with n=128 in, 32 out, 256 samples and measure timing. Keep timeout 300s.\n\nLet me write it carefully and cap runtime.\n\nThen, arm count table for feasibility:\nPrimary grid: 4 methods × 3 precisions = 12 quantization runs per model + 1 FP reference = 13 per model.\nModels: 3 primary (LLaMA-2 7B, 13B, LLaMA-3 8B) + tiny smoke (1.1B) = 4 → 4×13 = 52 runs.\nAblations: quantizer grid (sym vs asym) × group {128,64,32}: run 2 arms (RTN, GPTQ) × 2 rot × 3 bits... that's large; restrict ablation to one model (7B) and subset: e.g., 3 precisions × 2 methods × 2 rot × (1 sym config + 2 extra group sizes asym) = 36 runs. Plus 3 seeds for randomized Had arms (RTN+Had, GPTQ+Had) at each bit → 2 methods × 3 bits × 2 extra seeds = 12 extra. Plus online-vs-folded FP check (2 runs, no quant). Plus per-block ΔPPL sweep: reuse rotated/quantized checkpoints, ~ cheap forward passes.\nTotal ≈ 52 + 36 + 12 + 2 ≈ 100 quantization passes + evals.\n\nCompute estimate: per-run cost = quantization pass (one forward over calibration set + weight pass) + eval (PPL over WikiText-2/C4 ~ a few forward passes over 2048-token windows; zero-shot ~ 1-2 h for 7B on one A100). I'll give a rough estimate: GPTQ quantization on 7B ≈ 1–3 h on a single 80 GB GPU with 128×2048 samples; RTN ≈ minutes; PPL (WikiText-2 + C4) ≈ 0.2–0.5 GPU-h; zero-shot 6 tasks ≈ 1–2 GPU-h for 7B. So per arm ≈ 2–5 GPU-h for 7B; 100 arms × avg model factor... 13B ~ 1.8×, 70B ~ 6-9× (or 2×80GB). Estimate: ~400–800 GPU-hours for the primary grid + ablations excluding 70B; 70B optional adds ~300–600 GPU-hours. Present as order-of-magnitude, clearly labeled as planning estimate, not measurement.\n\nStorage: 7B fp16 ≈ 13.5 GB; 13B ≈ 26 GB; 8B ≈ 15 GB; 70B ≈ 140 GB; plus quantized artifacts small (~2-4 GB at 4-bit... actually 7B at 4-bit ≈ 3.5-4 GB). Total ~ 0.5–1 TB recommended; /workspace 11.3 GB free → insufficient.\n\nNow, the design must also address: \"operational definition of traditional-methods failure\": e.g., GPTQ-W2 PPL ≥ 5× (or 10×) FP16 PPL, or zero-shot avg ≥ X points below FP16, or PPL above a \"degenerate\" threshold (e.g., > 10,000 or non-finite). I'll pre-register: collapse := PPL_w2 / PPL_fp16 ≥ 5 OR zero-shot avg drop ≥ 15 points OR non-finite.\n\n2-bit stability claim: QuaRot+GPTQ W2 PPL ≤ 2× FP16 PPL on WikiText-2 AND zero-shot avg within 5 points of FP16 — but at 2 bits even QuaRot may be worse; paper reports W2 with group 128/64 as reasonably close for 70B but not for 7B. Since thresholds are proposed choices, I'll frame thresholds relative to observed RTN/GPTQ rather than absolutes, and offer a two-tier criterion (strong claim: within 2×; weak claim: ≥5× better than GPTQ baseline in PPL-ratio terms). Let me define:\n\n- Primary test (per model, per metric): Δ_QuaRot(b) = PPL(GPTQ+Had, b)/PPL(FP16), Δ_base(b) = PPL(GPTQ, b)/PPL(FP16). Claim \"QuaRot improves quality\" if Δ_QuaRot(b) < Δ_base(b) with paired bootstrap 95% CI excluding 0 difference in PPL, at each bit.\n- 2-bit stability (main yes/no): strong = Δ_QuaRot(2) ≤ 2.0 AND zero-shot avg within 5 pts; graded = Δ_QuaRot(2) ≤ 0.25 × Δ_base(2) (i.e., ≥4× reduction in excess PPL) but fails strong → \"improves but does not stabilize\".\n- Monotone trend: Spearman correlation across b∈{4,3,2} of benefit G(b) = log Δ_base(b) − log Δ_QuaRot(b) with pre-registered requirement ρ ≥ 0.8 / Jonckheere–Terpstra trend test p<0.05 (across models as replicates).\n- Falsification: