{"id": "1a47b5f0-8666-44eb-8caf-3e49d48c6bf5", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:27:57.336121", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "bdfd5324-7d4c-4d0d-ab08-4e53c4931db2", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Interesting: in this toy, my simplified GPTQ does not beat RTN — because the calibration set is tiny (ns=64 samples for n=64 dims, so H is rank-deficient, and my per-row shared scales + reverse sweep). This is a valuable *design* observation: with small calibration vs dimension, GPTQ's curvature estimate is unreliable and the error feedback can amplify error — i.e., the harness needs a **sanity gate**: GPTQ must beat RTN at 4/8-bit before trusting it; and calibration size must be large relative to dim. Also the toy's \"best arm = RTN+Had\" mirrors the design's prediction that after rotation, the simpler method gets most of the benefit — but I must be very careful not to overclaim from a toy.\n\nKey honest takeaways to carry into the design:\n1. Hadamard's benefit is real in the curvature-weighted objective too (x4.4–8.2 for RTN, x2.6–3.9 for my simplified GPTQ) and comes with a measurable mechanism signal: curvature-diag max/mean goes from ~34 to ~1.2 (uniformization) — a directly measurable mechanism metric in real models (input-channel Hessian diagonal non-uniformity / max|w|/σ).\n2. The toy GPTQ ≠ real GPTQ (no act-order, shared scales, tiny ns); its failure to beat RTN is a *harness-validation warning*: pre-register a gate \"GPTQ must beat RTN at W8/W4 in the harness before any W2 conclusion is trusted\".\n3. Negative control (no-outlier case ratio ≈ 1.0) → adopt as pre-registered negative control in the real experiment: apply the same Had-vs-noHad comparison on outlier-free layers (e.g., layers empirically without outliers, or a Gaussianized synthetic surrogate from measured stats) → expected no effect. Also a dose-response curve as a function of measured max|w|/σ per layer → predicted monotone relation. This is cheap and powerful (probe A2/A3 gives dose-response: ratio 1.0 → 0.07 as outlier frac/scale increases).\n4. Dose-response prediction: benefit largest at moderate outlier fraction (0.01) and shrinks when weights are *mostly* outliers (frac=0.2 → 0.611) — real models have moderate outliers, so predict largest gains at 7B/13B where σ ratio is large.\n\nAlso note the sign-flip detail: randomized Hadamard (random signs before H) matters — I used signs; and probe shows non-randomized Had alone (probe A1 with signs random). Should add ablation: deterministic Had vs randomized Had vs identity, plus a \"random orthogonal (dense)\" arm in the toy/real? A dense random orthogonal rotation is infeasible at 4096 dims for folding? Actually it is foldable offline (matrix multiply) but QuaRot uses Hadamard because it needs online application for activations in W4A4. For weight-only, dense random orthogonal would work offline. Good design point: compare \"Hadamard\" vs \"randomized Hadamard\" vs \"dense random orthogonal\" (weight-only, offline-only) — the researcher's point 3 asks to keep weights-only vs weights+act/KV as a control. My probes show the uniformization mechanism, so I can pre-register the prediction that for weight-only, the specific choice of orthogonal matrix matters little (any well-mixing rotation works), while Hadamard's fast-transform property is what makes the *activation* rotation cheap. That's an important conceptual point and a falsifiable arm.\n\nLet me quickly run a probe comparing deterministic Had vs randomized Had vs dense random orthogonal (small dims) in the toy to see whether weight-only benefit depends on rotation type — cheap (n=64, dense random orthogonal via Gram-Schmidt). This gives a defensible design prediction. Let me do it.\n\nAlso probe: is there anything about per-group quantization along the rotated dimension? In QuaRot weight-only, groups are along the input dimension after rotation (same layout). Fine.\n\nLet me run probe D: rotation type comparison + \"wrong rotation\" control (permutation