t(len(W)*frac_rows)\\n        tot_n=tot_r=0.0\\n        for i,row in enumerate(W):\\n            e_n=err([row],False,bits,g); e_r=err([row],True,bits,g)\\n            if i<m: tot_n+=e_n; tot_r+=e_r\\n            else:   tot_n+=e_n; tot_r+=e_n\\n        a+=tot_n/len(W); b+=tot_r/len(W)\\n    print(f\\\" rotated-layer-fraction={frac_rows}: MSE={b/3:.5f} (ratio vs none {b/a:.3f})\\\")\\nPY\\npython3 /tmp/d/pd.py\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.667, \"output\": \"b=4: identity=0.18176(1.00x)  perm=0.18176(1.00x)  signonly=0.20072(1.10x)  had=0.03076(0.17x)  randhad=0.03208(0.18x)  denseorth=0.06105(0.34x)\\nb=3: identity=1.12321(1.00x)  perm=1.12321(1.00x)  signonly=1.22006(1.09x)  had=0.14468(0.13x)  randhad=0.14826(0.13x)  denseorth=0.27797(0.25x)\\nb=2: identity=5.78351(1.00x)  perm=5.78351(1.00x)  signonly=6.89602(1.19x)  had=0.92034(0.16x)  randhad=0.95164(0.16x)  denseorth=1.54940(0.27x)\\n\\nPartial rotation: rotate only first HALF of layers (mirror of 'MLP-only vs all' ablation), b=2\\n rotated-layer-fraction=0.0: MSE=5.78351 (ratio vs none 1.000)\\n rotated-layer-fraction=0.25: MSE=4.08105 (ratio vs none 0.706)\\n rotated-layer-fraction=0.5: MSE=2.86441 (ratio vs none 0.495)\\n rotated-layer-fraction=0.75: MSE=1.71743 (ratio vs none 0.297)\\n rotated-layer-fraction=1.0: MSE=0.92034 (ratio vs none 0.159)\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "1a47b5f0-8666-44eb-8caf-3e49d48c6bf5", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent"}
{"id": "8ef16106-dd4e-4d0f-9dfe-4b0f527c18e5", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T16:29:00.180184", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "9e626c14-a5b8-40a1-8cd2-0d2299231f8a", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "Excellent findings. Interesting: dense random orthogonal is worse than Hadamard in this synthetic setting — that's counterintuitive (dense orthogonal makes Gaussian → also mixes). Hmm, actually Hadamard produces very uniform mixing (each output = equal-magnitude combination ±), which maximally minimizes max|w|; a random orthogonal rotation of a vector with a single big outlier produces random weights (variance spread), so max|w| ends larger. Actually Hadamard's ±1/√n structure gives all coefficients equal magnitude → the rotated vector entries have near-identical variance contributions, i.e., \"balanced\" mixing. So Hadamard > random orthogonal for this metric in a small-n regime. This is a nice, design-relevant insight: the choice of Hadamard specifically (balanced/unimodular orthogonal matrix) matters — hence an ablation \"random orthogonal (dense) vs Hadamard vs randomized Hadamard\" is a meaningful mechanism ablation, not a redundant control. Note small-n caveats.\n\nAlso: permutation is exactly neutral (identity-like) — great negative control (a \"structure-preserving\" reordering does nothing, so the effect is mixing, not reordering). Sign-only is slightly harmful (0.9-1.2x). Partial rotation: benefit accumulates roughly multiplicatively with the fraction of rotated layers (log-linear) → supports per-layer/per-block attribution analysis: ΔlogMSE per rotated fraction ≈ constant.\n\nAlso Probe C showed: in the synthetic curvature-weighted objective with outlier-heavy activation channels, my simplified GPTQ does NOT beat RTN (0.73–0.77 ratio means GPTQ worse) — because the synthetic curvature is dominated by a few outlier channels, and my toy GPTQ uses per-row scales computed before error feedback and reverse sweep. Important honest framing: in a synthetic toy, GPTQ-style error feedback can even hurt; that is exactly why the design must (a) validate the GPTQ implementation against a known-good reference (harness gate: GPTQ must beat RTN at 8-bit and 4-bit on a real model before trusting 2-bit numbers), and (b) report calibration size / damping. Good — I'll include a \"quantizer-harness acceptance gates\" section.\n\nAlso diag non-uniformity: 34.5 → 1.19 after Hadamard, showing the mechanism.\n\nI have enough probe evidence. Now write the final design document. It must include:\n- Scope & claims boundary\n- Section A: FIXED vs PROPOSED table\n- Section B: frozen primary configuration (g*, asymmetric, excluded layers) + to-confirm items\n- Section C: Rationale / mechanism model (why Hadamard should help; the quantization noise bound; orthogonality invariance; group scale interaction)\n- Section D: Experimental factors & arms (4×3 grid × N models + FP16 reference)\n- Section E: Controls & ablations (equivalence gate, weights-only vs +act/KV control, group size, sym/asym, rotation family incl. dense orthogonal & permutation & sign-only, partial rotation/leave-one-class-out, negative control outlier-free, seeds)\n- Section F: Metrics (primary PPL, zero-shot; mechanism metrics: per-layer quant MSE, max|w|/σ, kurtosis, curvature-diag non-uniformity, per-block ΔPPL)\n- Section G: Analysis plan + pre-registered decision rule incl. thresholds, trend test, falsification, operational definition of \"RTN/GPTQ fail\"\n- Section H: Tier-0 synthetic illustration (with my numbers, labeled synthetic) + harness acceptance gates\n- Section I: Feasibility: can/cannot run here; compute estimate formula; storage; external requirements\n- Section J: Assumptions & to-confirm items (marked)\n- Section K: Risks/limitations\n\nThresholds: define operational failure: GPTQ-W2 PPL > 5× FP16 PPL (or > 10×) = collapse; QuaRot stabilizes at 2-bit iff PPL(GPTQ+Had, W2) ≤ 2× FP16 PPL AND zero-shot avg within 5 pp of FP16... Note realistic: paper reports W2 LLaMA-2 7B QuaRot g=32 PPL ~ 20-ish vs FP 5.47? I don't know exactly. So better to define thresholds as ratios and pre-register both a strict and relaxed criterion. And be careful: define thresholds relative to FP16 and also require monotone benefit trend 4→3→2.\n\nCompute estimate: arms = (4 methods × 3 bits) + FP16 ref = 13 quant runs per model; seeds for randomized Had only (2 rotation seeds × 2 Had arms × 3 bits = extra 12 runs, or reuse); ablations: group size {64,32} × 4 methods × 2 bits (2,3) on 7B only = 16 runs; sym grid 4×3=12 on 7B; partial rotation ~ 2×(number of layer classes ≈ 6) = 12 PPL evals; per-block ΔPPL: 32 blocks × 2 arms = 64 forward-only evals. Total runs.\n\nCompute estimate table: GPTQ quantization on 7B ≈ 1–3 GPU·h on one A100-80G (calibration 128×2048), 13B ≈ 2–5 h, 70B ≈ 8–20 h (multi-GPU). PPL eval (WikiText-2 + C4, seq 2048) ≈ 0.1–0.3 GPU·h for 7B, more for 70B; zero-shot 6 tasks ≈ 0.5–1.5 GPU·h 7B. Rough total: 7B+13B+8B grid ≈ 13 runs × ~2.5 GPU·h avg = ~35 GPU·h quantization + ~65 GPU·h eval ≈ 100 GPU·h; 70B optional ≈ 300–500 GPU·h. Ablations +20–40%. Total primary ≈ 150 GPU·h (without 70B) / ~600 GPU·h with 70B. These are planning estimates labeled as such, not measurements.\n\nStorage: LLaMA-2-13B fp16 ~26GB, 7B ~13.5GB, LLaMA-3-8B ~15GB, 70B ~140GB; plus quantized artifacts (~2-4 GB each). /workspace has 11.3 GB free → insufficient even for one 7B fp16 checkpoint. Good honest point.\n\nI'll note arm count formula: N_runs = Σ_models [ (4 methods × 3 bits) + 1 FP ref + ablations ].\n\nLet me also state clearly the \"weights-only vs weights+activations/KV\" control: primary arms keep activations FP; control arm set C-W4A4KV4 only to attribute how much of the reported end-to-end gain in the paper comes from activation/KV rotation, run only on one model/one precision.\n\nAlso state: since for weight-only the rotation is folded offline, the online model math is identical → equivalence gate: FP16 rotated vs FP16 original PPL difference must be < 0.01 ppl (within float tolerance), and per-layer output max abs diff < tolerance; folded vs online (H applied at runtime) must agree.\n\nStatistical plan: bootstrap over eval documents for PPL CIs (paired same document windows across arms), zero-shot accuracy via paired binomial/bootstrap; variance across 3 random-rotation seeds reported as ±std, and a mixed model / permutation test; explicitly state PPL eval itself is deterministic given weights → variance comes only from rotation seed & (GPTQ is deterministic given calib subset; calib subset sampling is another variance source).\n\nAlso pre-register analysis unit: (model, bit) pairs as the cluster for trend test; Spearman correlation between (4-bits) bits and Hadamard benefit (ΔlogPPL) with monotonicity expectation positive.\n\nAlso: \"operational failure definition\": RTN/GPTQ fail at bit b if PPL_arm ≥ max(3× PPL_FP16, PPL_FP16 + 5) or zero-shot avg drop ≥ 10 pp, or ppl divergence (inf/nan).\n\nSuccess claim mapping:\n- Q1 \"how does Hadamard improve quality\" → answered by mechanism metrics + attribution ablations (factorial 2×2: rotation × method) → main effect of Hadamard, interaction with GPTQ (does GPTQ still add after rotation?).\n- Q2 \"can QuaRot stabilize at 2–4 bit where RTN/GPTQ fail\" → decision rule (a),(b),(c).\n\nInteraction/substitution hypothesis: if Hadamard alone removes most of the outlier problem, then GPTQ's marginal gain post-rotation shrinks → pre-register H_substitution: ΔGPTQ gain (relative error reduction over RTN) shrinks after rotation. My probe C showed exactly that in a synthetic setting (0.73→0.31 ratio... careful: those ratios mean GPTQ was worse than RTN both with and without rotation; but the \"extra GPTQ gain\" changed from 0.73 to 0.31 meaning GPTQ hurt more after rotation. Hmm — in my toy GPTQ hurt in both cases; that's a property of my toy implementation (per-row scales, reverse sweep, tiny calibration), so I must not use it to claim a substitution effect. I should report it honestly as: my toy GPTQ implementation did NOT beat RTN in the synthetic setting, which is itself why the design mandates a GPTQ implementation validation gate (must beat RTN at 4/8-bit on a real model) before any GPTQ-based conclusion. That's an honest and useful finding for design.\n\nGood. Now write final answer. It should be complete but organized. Length: substantial. Let's write it.\n\nFixed vs proposed table.\n\nFrozen primary config (pre-registered): \n- Per-group asymmetric uniform quantization (scale + zero-point), group size g* = 128 (to-confirm; requirement-to-confirm from setup topics), along input-channel dimension of each linear weight (out_features × in_features), one scale/zero-point per (row, group).\n- Quantized tensors: all linear layers in transformer blocks (q,k,v,o, gate, up, down). Excluded: input embeddings, lm_head (untied), all norms (RMSNorm), rotary embeddings untouched... plus optionally down_proj input rotation.\n- Hadamard: randomized Hadamard (sign flip then FWHM) folded offline, per input-dim block (full hidden dim for most matrices, per-head for q/k? and for down_proj the input dim is d_ff, so it needs a Hadamard of the intermediate dim — folded offline for weight-only, no online needed). Note this for weight-only, unlike W4A4, there's no need to keep an online Hadamard — flag as requirement-to-confirm because for activations FP, all rotations can be absorbed.\n- Calibration for GPTQ: 128 sequences × 2048 tokens from C4 train (not WikiText-2, to avoid eval leakage), act-order on, percdamp {0.01} fixed.\n- Eval: WikiText-2 test, seq len 2048, non-overlapping windows, mean NLL-based PPL; C4 validation same; zero-shot: PIQA, ARC-e, ARC-c, HellaSwag, WinoGrande, LAMBADA via lm-eval-harness, dtype fp16.\n- Seeds: 3 rotation seeds (randomized-Hadamard signs) → report mean±std; GPTQ calibration subset fixed.\n\nNow let me write.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "mkdir -p /tmp/e && cat > /tmp/e/gate.py <<'PY'\n# PROBE E: the four \"harness acceptance gates\" a real implementation must pass (synthetic, known-truth).\n# G1 exactness of rotation (FP no-quant => error exactly 0)   G2 orthogonality of R (R R^T = I)\n# G3 quantizer degeneracy at 8 bits (error -> ~0)             G4 permutation-invariance of group quant\nimport random, math\ndef fwhm(v):\n    n=len(v); h=1\n    while h<n:\n        for i in range(0,n,h*2):\n            for j in range(i,i+h):\n                a,b=v[j],v[j+h]; v[j]=a+b; v[j+h]=a-b\n        h*=2\n    s=1.0/math.sqrt(n)\n    for i in range(n): v[i]*=s\nn=64; r=random.Random(0)\nv=[r.gauss(0,1) for _ in range(n)]; signs=[1-2*(r.random()<0.5) for _ in range(n)]\nx=[a*b for a,b in zip(v,signs)]; fwhm(x)\ny=list(x); fwhm(y); y=[a*b for a,b in zip(y,signs)]\nprint(\"G1 round-trip max abs error:\", f\"{max(abs(a-b) for a,b in zip(y,v)):.2e}  (gate: < 1e-12)\")\nnorm=sum(a*a for a in x)**0.5\nprint(\"G2 norm preserved by R:\", f\"{norm/ (sum(a*a for a in v)**0.5):.12f} (gate: 1.0 +/- 1e-12)\")\ndef qdeq(row,bits,g,asym=True):\n    lv=2**bits-1; out=[0.0]*len(row)\n    for s in range(0,len(row),g):\n        blk=row[s:s+g]\n        if asym:\n            lo=min(blk); hi=max(blk); q=max((hi-lo)/lv,1e-8)\n            for i,val in enumerate(blk): out[s+i]=round((val-lo)/q)*q+lo\n        else:\n            m=max(max(blk),-min(blk)); q=max(m,1e-8)/(2**(bits-1)-1)\n            for i,val in enumerate(blk): out[s+i]=round(val/q)*q\n    return out\nW=[[r.gauss(0,1)+(25*r.choice([1,-1]) if r.random()<0.01 else 0) for _ in range(n)] for _ in range(n)]\nfor bits in (16,8,4):\n    e=sum(sum((a-b)**2 for a,b in zip(qdeq(row,bits,64),row)) for row in W)/(n*n)\n    print(f\"G3 bits={bits}: MSE={e:.3e}\")\nperm=list(range(n)); r.shuffle(perm)\nrow=W[0]; a=sum((p-q)**2 for p,q in zip(qdeq(row,2,64),row))\nb=sum((p-q)**2 for p,q in zip(qdeq([row[perm[i]] for i in range(n)],2,64),[row[perm[i]] for i in range(n)]))\nprint(f\"G4 permuted-input MSE before=