ed (to-confirm) |\n| Excluded tensors | embeddings, lm_head, RMSNorm/scale params, rotary params | proposed (to-confirm) |\n| Hadamard variant | **randomized Hadamard** (random ±1 diagonal then Sylvester H), folded **offline** into weights; no online Hadamard in primary arms | proposed |\n| GPTQ hyper-params | act-order on, percdamp 0.01, blocksize 128, calibration = C4, 128×2048 tokens | proposed |\n| Eval harness | WikiText-2 + C4 PPL at seq len 2048; zero-shot PIQA/ARC-e/ARC-c/HellaSwag/WinoGrande/LAMBADA | proposed |\n| Seeds | 3 random-sign seeds for the two \"+Hadamard\" arms; deterministic single run for RTN/GPTQ | proposed |\n| Decision thresholds (§F) | ×3 stability, ×10 collapse, monotone-trend floor | proposed (pre-registered) |\n\nEverything except the first five rows is a choice, tunable or replaceable, and is labeled as such throughout.\n\n---\n\n## B. Frozen primary configuration (design-level resolution of my biggest uncertainty)\n\nMy probe showed the *sign* of the Hadamard effect can flip with quantizer choice (symmetric 2-bit: rotation **worse** 3.3–5.2×; asymmetric 2-bit: rotation **better** ~12×). Therefore:\n\n**Pre-registration rule.** All four arms × three bit-widths × all models use **one identical quantizer config C\\***; primary conclusions come *only* from C\\*. Grid type (sym vs asym) and group size {g\\*, 64, 32} are demoted to a **formal mechanism ablation** (§D-A1/A2) whose results may explain, but never establish, the answer.\n\n**C\\* = {** per-group asymmetric min/max+zero-point uniform quantizer; groups contiguous along the input dimension; g\\*=128; quantize q,k,v,o,gate,up,down of every block; exclude embed/lm_head/norms; rotation = randomized Hadamard of matching order folded offline (X W = (XH)(HᵀW)) with the inverse applied to the preceding linear/norm so the FP function is mathematically unchanged; RTN = plain nearest rounding on C\\* grid (no clipping search); GPTQ = C\\* grid + Hessian above, **identical** whether or not rotation is applied; FP16 reference run; identical eval split/seed for every arm. **}**\n\nWhy asymmetric is the primary: at 2 bits a symmetric grid has only 3 usable levels and wastes one on zero, so the outlier-clipping effect dominates and the rotation's contribution is confounded — exactly what `[SYN]` showed.\n\n---\n\n## C. Primary grid (arms)\n\nPer model: **4 methods × 3 precisions = 12 arms + 1 FP16 reference**, with 3 sign-seeds on the two Had arms → 12 + (2 Had arms × 3 bits × 2 extra seeds) = **19 quantization artifacts per model**.\n\n| | RTN (no calib) | GPTQ (Hessian) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **no rotation** | A1 · 4/3/2 | A2 · 4/3/2 |\n| **+ randomized Hadamard (folded)** | A3 · 4/3/2 | A4 · 4/3/2 |\n\nThis 2×2 (rotation × compensation-method) × 3-bit design is what lets us answer *how* (interaction/mediation) and not just *whether*.\n\n## D. Controls and ablations\n\n**Correctness controls (gates — must pass before any result is read)**\n- **C1 Equivalence gate (the one you asked for):** FP16 + folded-Hadamard vs FP16 original must give **identical PPL** (target: ΔPPL within FP numerical noise, e.g. <0.1%); and \"online Hadamard\" vs \"offline folded\" must agree bit-for-bit. This proves any later ΔPPL is a quantization effect, not a transformation artifact. `[SYN]` support: round-trip error 4.4e-16, norm ratio 1.000000000000; quantizer MSE monotone in bits (16b 9.5e-9 → 8b 6.0e-4 → 4b 1.8e-1); group-quant MSE exactly permutation-equivariant (311.5729 = 311.5729).\n- **C2 Activation/KV control:** one extra arm per model, QuaRot-style W4+FP activations vs W4A4KV4. Kept *outside* the primary grid — the question is weight-only quality — its only role is to show that the weight-only claim does not secretly depend on the activation/KV rotations.\n\n**Pre-registered negative controls (cheap outlier-mediator tests)**\n- **N1 outlier-free condition (your suggestion, adopted):** repeat C\\* pipeline on outlier-free weights. Prediction: ratio ∈ **[0.95, 1.05]** (no benefit). `[SYN]`: ratio 1.000 at outlier-frac 0, and 1.004–1.009 in the outlier-free matrix control. *If N1 is not null, the harness is buggy and no conclusion is drawn.*\n- **N2 permutation control:** permute input channels only (same group layout). Prediction: exact no-op. `[SYN]` 1.00× at all bits → benefit must come from *mixing*, not reordering.\n- **N3 diagonal-sign control:** random ±1 signs without Hadamard. Prediction: no benefit / slight harm. `[SYN]` 1.09–1.19× (worse).\n- **N4 non-Hadamard orthogonal control:** dense random orthogonal rotation of the same order. `[SYN]` was intermediate (0.34× at 4b vs Had 0.17×) → \"any rotation\" is *not* equivalent to Hadamard; a flat-spectrum/Hadamard-specific claim requires this control.\n- **N5 alternative-mechanism control (most important discriminator):** RTN + MSE-optimal clipping search, and per-channel (SmoothQuant-style) scaling, both unrotated. If these match GPTQ+Hadamard within CI, the effect must be attributed to **dynamic-range/outlier suppression in general**, not to Hadamard specifically.\n\n**Mechanism ablations (explanatory only, 1 model — 7B — to bound cost)**\n- **A1 group size** {128, 64, 32} × 4 arms × W{2,4}: `[SYN]` benefit shrinks monotonically (2b ratio 0.071 → 0.152 → 0.315 → 0.686 as g falls to 16) → prediction: QuaRot's advantage is largest at coarse groups and fades as fine-grained scaling already absorbs outliers.\n- **A2 grid type** symmetric vs asymmetric at each bit (the sign-flip axis).\n- **A3 rotation scope / dose-response:** rotate 0/25/50/75/100% of layers, plus leave-one-class-out (attention-only, MLP-only, gate/up-only, down-only). `[SYN]` dose curve 1.000/0.706/0.495/0.297/0.159 → near-log-linear accumulation to be compared against the real cumulative curve.\n- **A4 randomized vs deterministic Hadamard** (separates outlier suppression from sign-randomization/aliasing effects).\n\n## E. Metrics\n\n**Primary (quality):** WikiText-2 PPL (decision metric) and C4 PPL, seq len 2048, identical windows across arms; reported as **PPL and ratio r = PPL/PPL_FP16**.\n**Secondary (quality):** 6-task zero-shot accuracy + mean; **Δ from FP16 in points** and % accuracy retained.\n\n**Mechanism metrics (what makes the \"how\" question answerable):**\n- M1 per-tensor relative weight error ‖W−Ŵ‖²/‖W‖² (rotation is Frobenius-invariant, so it is directly comparable across arms; measured after un-rotation, i.e. in the *original* basis).\n- M2 **outlier/dynamic-range mediators**: max|w|/σ, kurtosis, and the fraction of group MSE contributed by the top-1% weights, before vs after rotation.\n- M3 **curvature-weighted layer error** ‖X(W−Ŵ)ᵀ‖²/‖XWᵀ‖² — GPTQ's *own* objective, tested under rotation (implemented and validated in `[SYN]`).\n- M4 **curvature diagonal non-uniformity** max(diag H)/mean(diag H): `[SYN]` 34.5 → 1.19 after Hadamard. Design-level prediction: rotation *flattens the curvature spectrum*, so GPTQ's error-feedback has little left to exploit → **RTN+Had ≈ or > GPTQ+Had**. Testing this redundancy prediction is the sharpest part of the \"how\".\n- M5 **per-block ΔPPL curves**: quantize only block i (and only ¼-depth spans) → where damage accumulates.\n- M6 overhead sanity: folded rotation adds **zero** online FLOPs; verify measured latency of FP16-original vs FP16-rotated ≈ identical.\n\n## F. Analysis plan + pre-registered decision rule\n\nOperational definitions: **r = PPL(arm)/PPL_FP16** on WikiText-2. \"Traditional method fails\" ⇒ **r ≥ 5** at that bit (with zero-shot mean drop ≥ 10 points as corroboration); \"collapse\" ⇒ **r ≥ 100** or non-finite.\n\nStatistics: paired bootstrap over WikiText-2 document windows (arms share the eval set → paired resampling, 10k replicates) for CI on Δ log-PPL; per-model results reported separately (models are replicates, not pooled CIs); Holm–Bonferroni over the 3 pre-declared primary hypotheses; everything else exploratory.\n\n1. **Main effect + interaction.** Two-way 2×2 (Hadamard × compensation) per bit-width on Δlog-PPL. Report (i) Hadamard main effect per bit, (ii) the interaction: if Hadamard helps RTN more than GPTQ, the two mechanisms are redundant (see M4 prediction) — an answer to *how* it works, not just *that* it works.\n2. **2-bit stability (headline claim).** \"QuaRot stabilizes 2-bit weight-only\" is supported **iff GPTQ+Hadamard at W2 has PPL ≤ 3× FP16 on both WikiText-2 and C4 AND zero-shot mean within 5 points of FP16**, while at least one traditional arm at W2 satisfies the \"fails\" definition (r ≥ 5).\n3. **Monotone trend test.** Benefit B(b) = log r_no-Had(b) − log r_Had(b) over b ∈ {4,3,2}. Require B non-decreasing as bits shrink (Jonckheere–Terpstra / bootstrap-permutation, and Spearman ρ = 1.0) **and** B(2) ≥ 2·B(4) as a pre-declared effect floor. A headline of \"helps more at lower precision\" without monotonicity is not claimed.\n4. **Falsification conditions (explicit).**\n - If **GPTQ+Hadamard also collapses at W2** (r > 10 or non-finite), the stabilization claim **fails**, *no matter how much better it is than GPTQ alone*. The honest report becomes \"improves, does not stabilize\".\n - If the equivalence gate C1 fails, all results are void (transformation bug).\n - If N5 (clipping/per-channel scaling) matches GPTQ+Hadamard, the claim is downgraded to \"outlier/dynamic-range suppression helps\", not \"Hadamard specifically\".\n - If N1's ratio is outside [0.95, 1.05], the harness is declared invalid.\n5. **Secondary hypothesis to report either way:** redundancy of Hadamard with Hessian-based compensation (M4/A-curve + interaction sign), and the group-size fade-out (A1).\n\n## G. Feasibility\n\n**Cost model (arm-count × model-size × pass).** Per model: 19 quantization artifacts + 19 PPL evals + 19×6 zero-shot evals; GPTQ artifacts (12/model) dominate quantization cost, zero-shot dominates eval cost.\n- Mid models (1.1B/7B/8B/13B): 3 primary × 19 artifacts ≈ 57 GPTQ/RTN passes ≈ **120–200 GPU-h** + PPL/zero-shot ≈ **150 GPU-h**; per-block ΔPPL curves ≈ 40 GPU-h; ablations on 7B (A1–A4) ≈ 60 GPU-h → **≈ 400 GPU-h, ~1 week on 4×80 GB**.\n- 70B optional top-line (4 arms × W2 only + FP16 ref ≈ 5 artifacts) ≈ **300–500 GPU-h** (2–3 weeks on 8×80 GB).\n- Tier-0 `[SYN]`: 0.6–2.2 s per probe on CPU, no deps.\n\n**Can / cannot run in *this* workspace (measured).**\n- ✅ Can: Tier-0 synthetic mechanism + metric-implementation validation (pure stdlib, seconds) — done.\n- ❌ Cannot: nothing LLM-related runs here. No GPU; `torch`, `transformers`, `numpy`, `scipy`, `datasets`, `tqdm` all absent; network **disabled** (DNS fails for pypi/HF; `pip install numpy` errors) so deps cannot be added; no checkpoints, no calibration corpora.\n- ❌ Storage: **/workspace has only 10.87 GB free** — cannot hold even one 7B FP16 checkpoint (~13.5 GB); 70B would need ~140 GB.\n- ⚠️ `/tmp` is ~1 TB tmpfs but **did not persist between sandbox invocations** (observed twice) → any real run must write artifacts to a persistent volume, and scripts must be self-contained per invocation.\n- ⚠️ Python **3.14**: wheels for the torch/transformers/lm-eval stack of the QuaRot era do not exist for it → a real run needs a rebuilt 3.10/3.11 environment.\n\n**External resources a real run needs:** 4–8 × 80 GB GPUs; ≥2 TB persistent scratch; gated LLaMA-2/3 weights; C4 + WikiText-2; torch/transformers/accelerate/lm-eval-harness; a QuaRot-compatible rotation implementation validated by C1–C2 and N1–N3 before any headline number.\n\n## H. Synthetic illustration vs empirical LLM results — kept strictly apart\n`[SYN]` summary table (asymmetric, g = full group, 3 seeds): MSE ratio Had/no-rot = 0.12 / 0.09 / 0.08 at 4/3/2 bits; symmetric 2-bit = **3.3 (worse)**; dose-response to outlier magnitude scale 0→100 gives ratio 1.000 → 0.035; curvature-weighted output error at 2 bits RTN 0.550, RTN+Had 0.126; curvature diag non-uniformity 34.5 → 1.19.\n**These numbers exist to (i) prove the metric code behaves as intended, (ii) fix the direction/sign of the hypotheses and controls, and (iii) justify freezing C\\*. They carry no magnitude information about any LLM, and no expected PPL is claimed anywhere in this design.**\n\n## I. Requirements-to-confirm (not verifiable from local source — the workspace contains only `task.json`)\ng\\* value; symmetric-vs-asymmetric and zero-point conventions; exact excluded-tensor list (esp. whether lm_head is quantized in the weight-only tables); whether paper's W2/W3 weight-only arms use randomized Had and whether any online Hadamard remains; GPTQ calibration corpus/size and default act-order/damping; PPL seq-len/window/stride convention; zero-shot task list and few-shot counts; whether the paper tabulates RTN+Had and GPTQ+Had separately (my grid assumes it should). These map to the `setup_5/7/11/16/17` topics; until confirmed they are **assumptions, not facts**.", "evidence": "**Repository/environment (all directly observed this session):**\n- /workspace contains exactly one file, `task.json` (579 B). `track.md` is not a file — only `task.json.documents[\"track.md\"] = \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` `find / -maxdepth 6 -iname '*quarot*' -o -iname '*gptq*' -o -iname '*perplexity*'` → no hits: no QuaRot code, no RTN/GPTQ implementation, no model defs, no calibration data, no eval harness, no weights.\n- Imports of torch/transformers/numpy/scipy/datasets/tqdm → ModuleNotFoundError. Network blocked (gaierror for pypi.org, files.pythonhosted.org, huggingface.co); `pip install numpy` fails. gpu_enabled=false.\n- 224 cores (Xeon Platinum 8570), ~2.0 TB RAM, /tmp tmpfs 1006 GB, /workspace free **10.87 GB** of ~1.1 TB (99% used). /workspace writes persist across calls; /tmp files did NOT (verified twice) → basis for the storage/persistence statements in §G.\n\n**Synthetic probes (pure stdlib, seconds each; design illustration only, zero claims about LLMs):**\n- Sign-flip risk (motivates frozen config §B): symmetric g=128 2-bit ratio Had/no-rot = 3.32–5.23 (rotation 